General elections were held in [[Ghana]] on 7 December 2008.<ref>{{cite news |first=Will |last=Ross |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7763304.stm |title=Poll stakes high in oil-boom Ghana |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2008-12-02 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref> Since no candidate received more than 50% of the votes, a [[Two-round system|run-off election]] was held on 28 December 2008 between the two candidates who received the most votes, [[Nana Akufo-Addo]] of the governing [[New Patriotic Party]] and [[John Atta Mills]] of the opposition [[National Democratic Congress (Ghana)|National Democratic Congress]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7775789.stm |title=Ghana election to go to a run-off |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2008-12-10 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref> Mills was certified as the victor by a margin of less than one percent, winning the presidency on his third attempt.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7809451.stm |title=Opposition leader wins run-off poll |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2009-01-03 |access-date=2009-01-03}}</ref> It is to date the closest election in Ghanaian history.

==Background==
On 21 December 2006, former [[Vice-President of Ghana|Vice-President]] John Atta Mills, who unsuccessfully ran as the [[National Democratic Congress (Ghana)|National Democratic Congress]] (NDC) presidential candidate in [[2000 Ghanaian presidential election|2000]] and [[2004 Ghanaian presidential election|2004]], was overwhelmingly elected by NDC as its candidate for the 2008 presidential election.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.modernghana.com/GhanaHome/NewsArchive/news_details.asp?t=mg&id=VFZSSmQwNTZaM2c9&menu_id=1&sub_menu_id=0 |title=NDC CONGRESS RESULTS-Prof Wins |publisher=ModernGhana.com |date=2006-12-22 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref>

Former Foreign Minister Nana Akufo-Addo was elected as the 2008 presidential candidate of the governing [[New Patriotic Party]] (NPP) at a party congress on 23 December 2007. Although he fell short of the required 50%, the second-place candidate, former Trade Minister [[John Alan Kyeremanten]], conceded defeat and backed Akufo-Addo.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.modernghana.com/GhanaHome/NewsArchive/news_details.asp?menu_id=1&id=VFZSVmVFMTZUVE09 |title=Akufo-Addo Is The Man Of The Moment&nbsp;— President |publisher=ModernGhana.com |date=2006-12-24 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref>

The stakes of the election were raised by the discovery of oil in Ghana and an expectation for incoming oil revenues to begin in 2010.<ref name=BBC_results>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7804212.stm |title=Ghana opposition claims poll win |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2008-12-30 | access-date=2009-01-04}}</ref> Additionally, allegations of electoral fraud that resulted in violence following elections in [[2007 Kenyan presidential election|Kenya]] and [[2008 Zimbabwean presidential election|Zimbabwe]] and military [[coup d'état|coups d'état]]  in [[2008 Mauritanian coup d'état|Mauritania]] and [[2008 Guinean coup d'état|Guinea]] caused international [[election monitor]]s to hope the Ghanaian elections would refurbish the image of [[constitutional democracy]] in Africa.<ref name=BBC_results/>

==Presidential candidates==
The following eight candidates formally registered with the [[Electoral Commission of Ghana]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ec.gov.gh/node/69 |title=2008 Presidential Candidates |publisher=[[Electoral Commission of Ghana]] |access-date=2008-12-10 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110515065211/http://www.ec.gov.gh/node/69 |archive-date=2011-05-15 }}</ref>

*Kwabena Adjei&nbsp;– A managing director of a timber company representing the Reformed Patriotic Democrats
*[[Nana Akufo-Addo]]&nbsp;– The former Attorney General and Minister for Justice, [[Minister for Foreign Affairs (Ghana)|Minister for Foreign Affairs]], and a current member of [[Parliament of Ghana|Parliament]] representing the [[New Patriotic Party]] (NPP)
*Kwasi Amoafo-Yeboah&nbsp;– An independent candidate
*Emmanuel Ansah-Antwi&nbsp;– Representing the [[Democratic Freedom Party]] (DFP)
*[[Edward Mahama|Edward Nasigri Mahama]]&nbsp;– Representing the [[People's National Convention (Ghana)|People's National Convention]] (PNC)
*[[John Atta Mills]]&nbsp;– The former [[Vice-President of Ghana|Vice-President]] representing the [[National Democratic Congress (Ghana)|National Democratic Congress]] (NDC)
*[[Paa Kwesi Nduom]]&nbsp;– The former Minister for Economic Planning & Regional Cooperation, Minister for Energy, Minister for Public Sector Reform, and a current member of Parliament representing the [[Convention People's Party]] (CPP)
*Thomas Nuako Ward-Brew&nbsp;– Representing the [[Democratic People's Party (Ghana)|Democratic People's Party]] (DPP)

==Opinion polls==
A poll conducted in April 2008 showed Mills slightly ahead of Akufo-Addo. The National Commission for Civic Education conducted the poll which sampled 5,327 people. The poll also predicted a high voter turnout of 96.9%. Respondents came from coastal, middle and northern areas of the country.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.thinkghana.com/elections/polls/120.php |title=NCCE Poll&nbsp;— April 2008 |publisher=Think Ghana |access-date=2008-12-10 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081211050605/http://www.thinkghana.com/elections/polls/120.php |archive-date=2008-12-11 }}</ref>

Another poll conducted in October 2008 by the [[Angus Reid Global Monitor]] saw Akufo-Addo leading. The poll was conducted by interviewing 3,000 adults in all the [[Regions of Ghana|regions]] of the country.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32333/akufo_addo_leads_rivals_in_ghana |title=Akufo-Addo Leads Rivals in Ghana |publisher=[[Angus Reid Global Monitor]] |date=2008-12-01 |access-date=2008-12-10 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090111224554/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32333/akufo_addo_leads_rivals_in_ghana |archive-date=2009-01-11 }}</ref>

