==Results==
Turnout on election day was very high. Since few votes were expected for other candidates than those of the two largest parties, a first-round victory for Akufo-Addo or Mills was seen as possible, but Nduom stated he wished to "surprise" the other parties by gaining enough votes to force a run-off between the two others.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7771133.stm |title=Race tight in Ghana's vote count |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2008-12-08 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref> With 40% of the vote counted, Akufo-Addo was leading with 49.5% to Mills's 47.6%.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://rawstory.com/news/afp/Ghana_ruling_party_chief_holds_slim_12092008.html |title=Ghana ruling party chief holds slim vote lead for president |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |website=[[The Raw Story]] |date=2008-10-09 |access-date=2008-12-10}}{{dead link|date=May 2026|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}} </ref> While Mills pulled ahead afterwards, Akufo-Addo again led by a slim margin with over 70% of the votes counted.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7772707.stm |title=Ruling party's man leads in Ghana |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2008-10-09 |access-date=2008-12-10}}</ref>

The second round was rerun on 28 December 2008 but due to logistics problems, the [[Tain District]] alone had its run-off election on 2 January 2009 due to problems with distributing ballots. Following the voting on 28 December, Mills led by a slim margin, causing the Election Commission to state it would not announce Mills as the winner until after the election rerun in Tain.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5h-sSFDir6ZY-P1r-O9Qq3A7zgEoQD95D6R700 | title=Ghana presidential vote to be rerun in single area | agency=Associated Press | date=2008-12-30 | access-date=2009-01-04}}{{dead link|date=June 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Prior to the announcement hundreds of NDC supporters converged on the election headquarters demanding that Mills be declared the victor, but were kept at bay by riot police and armed soldiers.<ref name=BBC_results/>

Fear of election day violence caused the NPP to file a lawsuit seeking to delay voting in Tain as it claimed that "the atmosphere in the rural district was not conducive to a free and fair election". The court denied the NPP's injunction request and said it would only hear the case on 5 January 2009. In response, the NPP called its supporters to boycott the vote, for which it was criticised by civil groups.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/7807448.stm | title=Ghana vote despite party boycott | work=BBC News | date=2009-01-02 | access-date=2009-01-04}}</ref>

